Two changes needed: the `role="radiogroup"` fieldset itself needs `aria-invalid` and an `aria-describedby` link to the error text, and each child radio should get `aria-invalid` too (setting a custom `error` attribute alone doesn't reach the accessibility tree).

```js
updated(changedProperties) {
  if (changedProperties.has('error')) {
    // existing behaviour: mirror the error attribute onto each child,
    // plus expose invalid state to assistive tech via aria-invalid
    this.items.forEach((el) => {
      if (this.error) {
        el.setAttribute('error', '');
        el.setAttribute('aria-invalid', 'true');
      } else {
        el.removeAttribute('error');
        el.removeAttribute('aria-invalid');
      }
    });
  }
}

render() {
  const errorId = 'group-error';

  return html`
    <fieldset
      role="radiogroup"
      aria-invalid="${this.error ? 'true' : 'false'}"
      aria-describedby="${this.error ? errorId : nothing}"
    >
      <legend><slot name="legend"></slot></legend>
      <slot></slot>
      ${this.error
        ? html`<p id="${errorId}" role="alert" aria-live="assertive">${this.error}</p>`
        : nothing}
    </fieldset>
  `;
}
```

- `aria-invalid` on the `radiogroup` role announces the group's validity state to screen readers immediately when focus lands there, not just when the message first appears.
- `aria-describedby` ties the error text to the group so it's read out as part of the group's accessible description (not only relying on the transient `role="alert"` announcement, which some AT/verbosity settings suppress).
- Mirroring `aria-invalid` onto each child radio covers the case where a user tabs directly to an individual radio rather than the group.
